    "title": "What the Second Chord Is For",
    "summary": "The second chord of a modal vamp is the one that contains the characteristic note, and everywhere but Lydian's the home chord's quality has to finish the job.",
    "tags": ["modes", "theory", "ear"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every vamp in this pathway opens on a chord you can stand on \u2014 one with a perfect fifth over its root, solid enough to be home. That much was settled before you got here: Lydian stands on "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", Dorian on "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and so on through every mode with a home that holds. What happens next is what actually decides the mode. The second chord isn't picked at random, and you've already met it built the same way five separate times without a name for the pattern behind it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The second chord of a modal vamp is the one that contains the characteristic note."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "It's the only place in a two-chord loop where the harmony itself asserts that note, rather than leaving it to whatever you happen to reach for while soloing over the top. Play the first chord alone and you've stated home; add the second and you've stated what makes this particular home sound the way it does. Four of the seven modes satisfy the rule outright, and a fifth is worth setting beside them for what it reveals a moment later:"
        }
      ]
    },
